// expect-error: T282
//
// T282 — a `lo..=hi` match pattern whose bounds are not integer literals
// is rejected at type-check.
//
// The parser reaches its range arm for `BoolLit | IntLit | StrLit` alike,
// because range patterns share one arm with ordinary literal patterns.
// That makes `"a"..="z"` a well-formed `Pattern::Range` carrying two
// `Literal::Str` bounds. Nothing downstream wanted them: a range lowers
// to a pair of `>=` / `<=` comparisons, and ordering is defined only over
// the machine integer types.
//
// Two upstream checks each had a reason not to fire. The bound/scrutinee
// compatibility check compares each bound's type against the scrutinee's,
// and a `str` bound against a `str` scrutinee is compatible, so it passed.
// T190 (`lo > hi`) is gated on `(Literal::Int, Literal::Int)`, so a
// non-integer pair skipped it.
//
// (The compatibility code is deliberately NOT named here. It sits in the
// direct-test gap manifest, and the coverage census counts a bare code
// token in any test-tree file as a reference — so naming it in a comment
// would mark it "covered" without adding a single test for it.)
//
// AIR then coerced both bounds to `0` through a `_ => 0` arm, leaving a
// `Ptr >= I64` comparison that killed the build at the wasm backstop with
// `ICE: unsupported binary op GtEq for type Ptr`. Fail-closed, but the
// panic named the backend for what is a plain source-level mistake, and
// the house rule is that an ICE backstop should be unreachable because
// some upstream pass fenced it. T282 is that fence; the AIR arms are now
// backstops that name it.
//
// The MUTATION_SITE below is the pair of string bounds. Replace it with
// an integer range (`48..=57`) and change the parameter to `i64` and the
// fixture compiles cleanly — proving this pins the bound-kind check and
// not some incidental property of matching on a `str`.
module main;

fn classify(s: str) -> i64 {
    match s {
        // MUTATION_SITE
        "a"..="z" => { return 1; },
        _ => { return 0; },
    }
}
